      "worked": "For $a,b>0$ with $a,b\\ne1$, and $c>0$, change the second logarithm to base $a$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_a(b)\\log_b(c)&=\\log_a(b)\\frac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_a(b)}\\\\\\log_a(b)\\log_b(c)&=\\log_a(c).\\end{align*}$$",
